**Read:** [Petrology — Stephen A. Nelson, Tulane EENS 2120](../sources/Petrology-Nelson-Tulane.lingua#r_1MNEKH4WW2GQRD4FZWNRD1GDPZ), the complete thermodynamics and phase-diagram notes; [Petrology — Timothy Grove, MIT 12.109](../sources/MIT-Petrology-12-109.lingua#r_6Q1GZJKSZXQBM4N19CTRYG64TG), the full thermodynamics/phase-diagram lectures and exercises; [HPx-eos and THERMOCALC — models, data, and interpretation](../sources/HPx-eos-and-THERMOCALC.lingua#r_0QYHHGNWRA8RQEM5MADWXDRQFJ), the complete basic equilibrium/model explanation; [General chemistry](../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/General-chemistry.lingua#r_206GS6CVXD8WSY1VX7PRF7136V), Gibbs energy, equilibrium, activities, and constraints as needed.

A petrological equilibrium calculation needs system components, amounts, phases/models, constraints, and thermodynamic data. At specified temperature and pressure a closed system minimizes appropriate Gibbs energy subject to mass balance and admissible phases. An open fluid or other externally constrained component changes the problem.

Chemical potential is the change in Gibbs energy associated with a component amount under defined constraints. Coexisting phases must satisfy the relevant equality/reaction conditions. Bulk composition controls phase amounts and feasible assemblages; it is not identical to the composition of every phase.

Reference states, units, component definitions, oxidation state, and activities belong to the model. A phase label in two software databases need not imply identical endmembers or mixing behavior. Numerical minimization can be correct for data/models that are inappropriate to the sample or conditions.

**Work:** derive a simple two-phase exchange or reaction equilibrium using chemical potentials and mass balance. Identify independent components and distinguish an imposed chemical potential from a conserved amount. Compare a closed anhydrous calculation with a fluid-buffered version, explaining which observations could support either approximation for a real rock.

Read the full petrological thermodynamics notes and model explanations, following shared chemistry preparation where needed. Continue through [Interpret vapor pressure, phase change, and a phase diagram](../../../../stem/chemistry/general-chemistry/states/Phase-changes-and-equilibrium-maps.lingua#r_7XXDG9YQTWEGSDFX1HXPBTGY8S) for the shared phase-diagram foundation before the multicomponent phase rule and composition sections.

